Related to that, I would like to point out that there was a small bug in JAGS 4.1 and the truncation operator did not work correctly in the example given. With the recently released JAGS 4.2 this is corrected.

Best regards  --- Marc

Von: Kery Marc
Gesendet: Montag, 29. Februar 2016 22:04
An: hmec...@googlegroups.com
Betreff: Memo on Censoring and Truncation

Dear all,

I compiled the attached memo on the modeling of censoring and truncation in the BUGS language (in the JAGS incarnation) which I would like to share with you.

In short, censoring means that some data points have the interpretation of "at most XX", "lying somewhere between XX and XXX" or "at least XX". The associated forms of censoring are called left-, interval- and right-censoring. With censoring, you observe all data points, but in a sense some are observed with less "resolution". In contrast, with truncation, some data points cannot be observed: those which lie beyond the truncation limit.

All this can be modelled relatively easily in the BUGS language, as the document shows. Hope this is useful.

Best regards  --  Marc

